{"description":"M2 (sto:path-scoped-projection) delivery spike. Does a generated, path-scoped nested entry point (D/AGENTS.md derived from the binding index) resolve an agent's governing constraints better than the root-only block, and at what cost? Measure on THIS repository's own code tree so the projection decision has evidence, not taste.","investigationNotes":"Method — deterministic dogfood proxy over the eka-cli repository, NOT an LLM-driven A/B (no task harness was available; no experiment was fabricated). Task = \"resolve the constraints governing a given code file\". Population: 96 tracked non-test Go files under cmd/, bootstrap/, feedback/, scripts/.\n\nArm A (root-only, Standard before M2): the root AGENTS.md carries a flat, repo-global index (3,167 B block). Per-file resolution requires a targeted query, `eka context --for \u003cfile\u003e --json`.\n  - steps: 1 call per file (96 calls to cover the tree).\n  - per-file response: median 1,162 B; total 119,374 B for the tree.\n  - governing set per file: median 3 (min 2, max 5); 96/96 carry \u003e=1.\n\nArm B (projected, M2): each directory that directly holds tracked files and has non-empty bindings gets D/AGENTS.md naming its subtree's constraints (the block the agent loads on entering the directory). After the sealed-tree fix (v1.27.1) the eka-cli repo projects 8 nested files: bootstrap, cmd, cmd/eka, cmd/ui, feedback, scripts, scripts/pin-vendored, templates/hooks (root untouched; 0 files under exchange/snapshots).\n  - steps: 0 extra calls (the block is in context when the agent works in the directory).\n  - cost: 8 blocks, 8,596 B total (median 1,454 B/file) to cover the whole 96-file code tree.\n  - coverage vs ground truth: 96/96 files covered; index-vs-file-set EXACT=34, SUPERSET=62, SUBSET=0, DISJOINT=0 — the directory block is never missing a governing constraint.\n  - granularity: the directory index median is 18 constraints where the file's own set is median 3.\n\nCross-check: the coarse index is the strict union over the directory (every ADR whose scope touches a file under it), so a file under cmd/ sees ~18 constraints when ~3 apply.","conclusion":"Honest outcome: POSITIVE on coverage and steps, NEUTRAL-to-coarse on precision.\n\n- Coverage/resolution rate: Arm A gives 0% FILE-LOCAL resolution (the root block is repo-global, not path-scoped; the agent must issue a query); Arm B gives 100% file-local coverage and never misses a governing constraint (0 subset, 0 disjoint across 96 files).\n- Steps: 1 query/file (96 to cover the tree) -\u003e 0. The strongest, unambiguous win.\n- Cost: to cover the full 96-file code tree, Arm A = 119,374 B of query responses; Arm B = 8,596 B of standing directory blocks (~14x less), and the block is loaded once per directory, not per file.\n- Precision (the honest caveat): at directory granularity the block over-names — median 18 constraints named vs median 3 that actually govern a file (34/96 exact, 62/96 superset). This is exactly the failure mode the M1 spike (spk:path-binding-noise) flagged: a scope wide enough to be safe is wide enough that an agent may learn to skim it.\n\nDecision: ship the projection (v1.27.0, sealed-tree fix v1.27.1) as a PASSIVE, zero-step safety net — it guarantees an agent entering a directory holds every constraint that could bind, which the root-only world left to discipline. It is NOT a substitute for `eka context --for` when precise per-file resolution is required. Neutral-to-positive; no claim beyond the measured numbers."}